      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i keep getting an error when i try to run this macro for my monthly reports. \n\nrun-time error '1004': application-defined or object-defined error\n\nhere is the code:\n\nSub ReconcileReport()\n    Dim ws As Worksheet\n    Set ws = ThisWorkbook.Sheets(\"Monthly_Recon\")\n    \n    ws.Range(\"A1\").Value = \"Date\"\n    ws.Range(\"B1\").Value = \"Amount\"\n    \n    ' this part is where it keeps crashing\n    ws.Select\n    ws.Range(\"C1:C100\").SpecialCells(xlCellTypeBlanks).Value = 0\n    \n    MsgBox \"Done!\"\nEnd Sub",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A run-time error '1004' when trying to use `SpecialCells(xlCellTypeBlanks)` on a range like `C1:C100` usually means that *no* blank cells were found in that range. If there are no blanks, the macro throws this error because it can't find the specific cells to target. You might need to check if the range actually contains any empty cells, or perhaps use a loop instead if you need to process every cell regardless of its current state.\n\nTry wrapping that line in an `On Error Resume Next` block temporarily to see if the code continues past the error, which would confirm the \"no blanks\" issue. Alternatively, if you want to set *every* cell in that range to zero, you can simply use `ws.Range(\"C1:C100\").Value = 0`.\n\nThis is a common snag, and it's almost always because the expected condition (finding a blank cell) isn't met. If you're expecting to find blanks, make sure your data entry hasn't accidentally filled those spots with spaces or zero values that Excel doesn't count as truly blank.\n\nIf you'",
