      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks that makes sense. i think i'll try the 'on error resume next' thing first to see if it works. \n\none more thing though, i noticed sometimes the error just happens randomly even when there are clearly empty cells. like one day it works and the next it doesn't. is there anything else that could be causing it besides just no blanks found?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Random errors, even when you see empty cells, are often caused by hidden data or environmental hiccups in Excel. It's quite common for a cell that *looks* blank to actually contain a hidden character, a formula returning an empty string (`\"\"`), or even a formatting ghost that confuses the `SpecialCells` method.\n\nWhen you're dealing with unpredictable behavior, it's usually best to be more explicit with your operations, much like when you're trying to set a specific type of target for a line of code. You can try looping through the range cell by cell instead of relying on `SpecialCells`.\n\nFor example, you could iterate from row 1 to 100 and check each cell individually:\n\n```vba\nDim i As Long\nFor i = 1 To 100\n    If IsEmpty(ws.Cells(i, \"C\").Value) Then\n        ws.Cells(i, \"C\").Value = 0\n    End If\nNext i\n```\n\nThis loop method is much more robust against those sneaky, invisible characters that cause the '1004' error. It's a reliable way to ensure you are only acting on truly empty",
      "source": "target",
